    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A finished epoxy floor looks completely done about twelve hours after the last coat goes down. It is glossy, it is hard to the touch, and it is nowhere near ready for a car. The chemistry that gives epoxy its toughness keeps running for days after the surface stops feeling wet, and driving on it before that chemistry finishes is the single most common way a well-installed floor gets wrecked in its first week.
        </p>

        <div id="dry-vs-cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Drying and Curing Are Not the Same Thing</h2>
          <p>
            Latex paint dries. Water leaves the film, the resin particles coalesce, and what remains is the paint. Nothing chemical happens after that.
          </p>
          <p>
            Epoxy cures. Mixing part A with part B starts an exothermic reaction in which the resin and hardener build a cross-linked polymer network, and that reaction continues long after the surface feels solid. The floor gets harder, more chemical resistant and more abrasion resistant every day of that period. A one-day-old coating may already be firm enough to walk on and still be soft enough that a hot tire will bond to it and pull it off the slab.
          </p>
          <p>
            That distinction explains every waiting period in this guide. Tack free means the surface has skinned. Walk-on means the film can take foot pressure without printing. Recoat means the surface still has active chemistry for the next layer to bond into. Full cure means the reaction has essentially finished and the coating has its published properties. They arrive in that order, hours to weeks apart.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">At 70 Degrees, Standard Epoxy</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8-12 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tack free</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">18-24 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Light foot traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5-7 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Vehicle traffic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#FF4500]">7-30 days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Full chemical cure</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. The Full Timeline, Hour by Hour</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">0 to 45 Minutes: Pot Life</h3>
          <p>
            The clock starts the moment part A meets part B, not the moment the material hits the floor. Most homeowner epoxies give 30 to 45 minutes of working time at 70 degrees, and heat shortens it sharply — material left in the pail generates its own heat and can gel in a fraction of the stated time. Pouring the mixed material out into a ribbon on the floor immediately buys working time, because a thin spread sheds heat that a deep bucket traps.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2 to 6 Hours: Flake and Broadcast Window</h3>
          <p>
            On a decorative floor, vinyl flake has to be broadcast while the base coat is still wet enough to grab it. That window is typically the first two to six hours. Broadcast too late and the flake sits on the surface instead of embedding, and the topcoat will not lock it down.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">8 to 12 Hours: Tack Free</h3>
          <p>
            The surface no longer transfers to a gloved finger. Dust that lands after this point mostly brushes off rather than embedding. The film is still soft underneath, and a foot placed on it will leave a print.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">12 to 24 Hours: Recoat Window and Walk-On</h3>
          <p>
            This is the window for the next coat, and for careful stocking-footed inspection. Walking on it in shoes with grit in the tread is a bad idea even once it holds your weight, because the film is soft enough to take an impression.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2 to 3 Days: Furniture and Light Loads</h3>
          <p>
            Shelving, tool chests and workbenches can go back, ideally lifted rather than dragged and set on pads. Point loads on steel feet will still emboss a coating at this stage.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5 to 7 Days: Vehicle Traffic</h3>
          <p>
            The earliest most standard epoxies should see a car, and there is no benefit in testing the lower bound. If the garage has been cold, extend it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">7 to 30 Days: Full Chemical Cure</h3>
          <p>
            The coating reaches its published hardness and chemical resistance. Until then, keep solvents, de-icing salt, wet cleaning and rubber mats off it. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-epoxy-garage-floor/">cleaning an epoxy garage floor</a> covers the routine that starts once this period ends.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="by-product" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Times by Coating Type</h2>
          <p>
            Chemistry changes these numbers more than brand does. The figures below assume 70 degrees and moderate humidity.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[600px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coating</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Walk-On</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drive-On</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Full Cure</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based epoxy kit</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-16 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 days</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">100% solids epoxy</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-24 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 days</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-30 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Polyaspartic</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-8 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24-48 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-part urethane topcoat</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-24 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-5 days</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One-part garage floor paint</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-12 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-7 days</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 days</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            The polyaspartic row is why one-day coating crews exist: a full system can go down in a morning and take a car the next day. The trade-off is a pot life measured in minutes rather than an hour. Our comparison of <a href="/epoxy-vs-polyaspartic-garage-floor/">epoxy versus polyaspartic</a> covers where that speed is worth the working difficulty.
          </p>
          <p>
            The last row deserves a warning. One-part latex garage floor paints feel dry quickly and take a full month to reach their final hardness, and parking on them at day four is the classic route to hot tire pickup. If you have used a paint rather than a real coating system, be more patient, not less.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Temperature and Humidity Change Every Number</h2>
          <p>
            Cure is a chemical reaction, and reaction rate is governed by temperature. The practical rule is that the rate roughly halves for every 18 degrees Fahrenheit of cooling. A coating that walks on in 24 hours at 70 degrees needs roughly two days at 52 degrees, and four at 34 — if it cures at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            The critical detail is that <strong>slab temperature</strong> governs, not air temperature. A concrete slab has enormous thermal mass and lags days behind the weather. Running a heater for three hours on an October morning warms the air to 70 while the floor sits at 48, and the epoxy cures against the cold surface. Measure the slab with an infrared thermometer, and if you are heating a cold garage, do it for a day or two before coating rather than an hour before.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[520px]">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Slab Temperature</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Walk-On</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Drive-On</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">85 degrees</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-14 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3-4 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">70 degrees</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18-24 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-7 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">60 degrees</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30-40 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-10 days</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">50 degrees</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">48-72 hr</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-14 days</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Humidity matters in a different way. High humidity does not slow the reaction much, but moisture condensing into an uncured film causes amine blush — a greasy, cloudy layer on the surface that interferes with the next coat and sometimes stays tacky. The rule is to keep the slab at least five degrees above the dew point through application and the first several hours of cure. In a humid climate, a dehumidifier running in a closed garage is genuinely useful.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Cold Weather Products Exist</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              If you must coat in a cold garage, buy a winter-grade formulation rather than trying to force a standard product to cure. Cold-cure epoxies use different hardeners and are rated down to 35 or 40 degrees. Adding solvent to thin a standard epoxy in the cold is not a workaround — it lowers film strength and can leave the floor permanently soft.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="recoat" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Recoat Window Is the One Deadline That Matters</h2>
          <p>
            Every multi-coat system has a window during which the previous layer is cured enough to walk on and still chemically active enough to bond with the next. Typically that is 12 to 24 hours at 70 degrees, and it stretches in the cold and shrinks in the heat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Hitting it means the two layers cross-link into what is effectively one film. Missing it means the surface has fully reacted, and the new coat can only bond mechanically — which requires abrading the whole floor with an 80 to 120 grit screen first. That is half a day of extra work with a rented buffer, and it is why professional crews schedule a floor around the recoat window rather than around the weekend.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plan the sequence before you start. On a Friday-evening base coat at 70 degrees, Saturday morning is the topcoat. If the forecast says the garage will be 55 degrees overnight, that window moves to Saturday afternoon or evening — and if you are not going to be there, it is better to plan on sanding than to guess. The full recoating procedure is covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-seal-epoxy-garage-floor/">sealing an epoxy garage floor</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="parking" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. When You Can Actually Park On It</h2>
          <p>
            Five to seven days at 70 degrees for a standard epoxy system, and every extra day helps. This is the number people push, and it is the number that costs them the floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            The failure mode is hot tire pickup. A tire coming off a highway run can be well over 130 degrees, and the plasticizers in warm rubber soften a coating that has not fully cross-linked. The tire cools, bonds, and when the car moves it lifts a patch of coating with it. On a fully cured floor the same tire does nothing. On a four-day-old floor it can leave a permanent scar in the shape of the contact patch.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two habits reduce the risk in the first weeks. Let the car cool before pulling it in — twenty minutes in the driveway after a long drive makes a real difference. And leave the car parked slightly differently for the first month rather than putting the same warm tires on the same two spots every day.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do not put rubber-backed mats, cardboard or plastic sheeting on a fresh floor as protection either. Anything that traps solvent vapor against a curing film keeps that spot from curing and can print a permanent pattern. If you must cover something, use a breathable drop cloth, and only after the floor is walkable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="problems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. When Cure Goes Wrong</h2>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Still Tacky After 48 Hours</h3>
          <p>
            Four likely causes: the ratio was off, the mix was incomplete, the temperature was below the product minimum, or moisture got into the film. Warm the garage to 75 or 80 degrees and run a fan for several days — a marginally cold cure often finishes given heat and time. If the floor is genuinely soft or sticky rather than slightly tacky, the chemistry failed and the coating has to come off. There is no additive that rescues it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soft Spots in an Otherwise Hard Floor</h3>
          <p>
            Almost always unmixed material from the sides or bottom of the pail that got rolled onto the floor. Scrape those areas back to sound coating, abrade the edges, and patch.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Greasy, Cloudy Film</h3>
          <p>
            Amine blush from moisture during cure. It usually washes off with warm water and a light abrasive pad, and the floor underneath is fine. What it cannot do is stay there — a topcoat applied over blush will not bond.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Blisters Appearing on Day Two or Three</h3>
          <p>
            This is not a cure problem, it is a moisture problem in the slab pushing vapor into the back of the film. The coating will not recover, and the fix is removal plus a moisture-mitigating primer — which is why testing first matters so much. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-prepare-concrete-for-epoxy/">preparing concrete for epoxy</a> covers the tests that catch this before you coat.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does garage floor epoxy take to dry?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At 70 degrees, most two-part epoxies are tack free in 8 to 12 hours, safe to walk on in 18 to 24 hours, ready for furniture in about three days, and ready for vehicle traffic in five to seven days. Full chemical cure takes 7 to 30 days depending on the product.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can park on an epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Five to seven days at 70 degrees for a standard epoxy, and longer if the garage is cold. Parking early is the most common way a new floor is ruined, because a warm tire on a partially cured coating causes hot tire pickup that lifts the film. Polyaspartic topcoats can usually take traffic in 24 to 48 hours.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does temperature affect epoxy cure time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dramatically. As a rule of thumb, cure rate roughly halves for every 18 degree drop in temperature, so an epoxy that walks on in 24 hours at 70 degrees may need 48 hours at 50 degrees and may never cure properly below its minimum application temperature. What matters is slab temperature, not air temperature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my epoxy floor still tacky?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual causes are an off-ratio mix, incomplete mixing, application below the minimum temperature, or moisture condensing into the film during cure. A slightly tacky floor may still harden if warmed and ventilated for several days. Persistently soft or sticky epoxy never cures and has to be scraped or ground off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long between epoxy coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Follow the recoat window on the data sheet, typically 12 to 24 hours at 70 degrees. Coating inside the window gives a chemical bond with no sanding. If you miss it, the surface must be abraded with an 80 to 120 grit screen so the next coat has a mechanical key.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you speed up epoxy curing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Warm the space and keep air moving. Bringing the garage to a stable 75 to 80 degrees with a heater and a fan meaningfully shortens cure, and a dehumidifier helps in humid weather. Never point a direct flame heater at a curing floor, and never add solvent to thin the mix — both cause more problems than they solve.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before you can wash a new epoxy floor?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wait until full chemical cure, typically seven days minimum and up to 30 for some systems. Water and cleaners on a partially cured film can leave permanent haze or soft spots. Dust mop as needed in the meantime and keep any wet cleaning off the floor until the manufacturer's cure period has passed.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Timing Mistakes That Cost a Floor</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking on Day Three</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The film feels rock hard and is not. A warm tire on a partially cured coating leaves a scar shaped like the contact patch, and the repair is a spot grind and recoat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging by Air Temperature</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heater warms air in an hour and a slab in a day. Check the concrete itself with an infrared thermometer before you decide a floor is ready for the next step.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Recoat Window Unintentionally</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sleeping through it turns a simple second coat into a full sanding job. Set an alarm and check the data sheet, because the window moves with temperature.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Covering a Fresh Floor to Protect It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plastic, cardboard and rubber mats trap solvent against the film and prevent cure underneath, leaving a permanent print of whatever you laid down.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">10. A Realistic Weekend Schedule</h2>
          <p>
            Most homeowners have two days, and the coating does not care about that. Here is how a standard two-coat epoxy system fits into a real calendar when the garage sits at a steady 70 degrees.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Saturday morning</strong> is prep: grinding, vacuuming, filling cracks and letting the patch material cure. <strong>Saturday afternoon</strong> is the base coat, mixed in batches and rolled out with flake broadcast into the wet film if the floor is decorative. By Saturday evening the surface is tack free and the garage should be closed against dust and insects, with a fan running for ventilation but not blowing directly across the film.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sunday morning</strong> lands inside the recoat window, which is where the clear topcoat goes down. That timing is the reason to start on Saturday rather than Sunday — a Sunday base coat pushes the topcoat to Monday morning, and most people cannot be there. By Sunday evening the floor is tack free again and the project is essentially finished from a labor standpoint.
          </p>
          <p>
            Everything after that is waiting. <strong>Tuesday or Wednesday</strong>, shelving and tool chests can move back in if they are lifted rather than dragged. <strong>The following weekend</strong>, roughly seven days after the topcoat, the car comes back in — cooled off in the driveway first. Two weeks out, the coating is hard enough for wet cleaning, and by the end of the month it has essentially everything it will ever have.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the forecast for that week includes a cold snap, shift the whole schedule rather than compressing it. A garage that drops to 50 degrees on Sunday night has effectively doubled every remaining interval, and the floor will tell you about it in the wheel paths a month later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Epoxy Curing Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every epoxy floor that fails in its first year was rushed at one of the three waiting points: between coats, before foot traffic, or before the car came back.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Confusing Walk-On Dry With Full Cure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy is walkable in about a day and fully cured in five to seven. In between it is soft enough to mark, and a floor that gets used at day two carries those marks permanently. The cure schedule is not padding — it is the resin still cross-linking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Missing the Recoat Window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A second coat applied inside the manufacturer's window bonds chemically to the first. Applied after it, the bond is only mechanical and the layers can separate cleanly later. Read the recoat times before planning the weekend, because they constrain the whole schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating in Cold Conditions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy cure is temperature dependent, and below roughly 60°F it slows dramatically or stalls, leaving a soft, tacky floor that never hardens properly. An unheated garage in late autumn is one of the most common causes of a floor that will not cure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking on It Too Early</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hot tyres pull at a coating that has not finished cross-linking, lifting circular patches where the tyres sat. This is the single most common DIY epoxy failure. Wait the full seven days before the car returns, even if the floor feels rock hard on day three.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

